markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Types of Loops

#### Many programs don’t just run one step and stop. They repeat actions over and over—checking for new input, updating a screen, or responding to a user. 

#### This repeated execution of instructions is called **iteration**, and it’s a core idea behind how web apps and programs work.

#### Before we look at how iteration appears in code, let’s ground the idea in a familiar situation.

---

<img src="https://images.code.org/481a5b8b1e9576dd3a25de4e063dc319-image-1768428984811.png" alt="basketball going through hoop" width="250px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 30px 40px 40px; transform: scaleX(-1);">


## Hoop, there it is! 


#### Imagine you are practicing your free throw shot.

#### **Round 1:**
#### You take exactly five free throws.

#### **Round 2:**
#### You keep tossing the basketball until you make one shot, no matter how many tries it takes.

#### 👉**Which round do you think would take longer and why?**


<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
    <summary style="font-size: 1.5em; font-weight: bold;">💡 Think about it:</summary>
<div style="margin-top: 20px; margin-left: 20px; margin-right: 20px; margin-bottom:75px;">
    <p style = "font-size: 14pt;">
    <ul>
    <li style = "font-size: 14pt;">In one case, you already know how many times you’ll repeat the action.</li>
    <li style = "font-size: 14pt;">In the other, you don’t know how many tries it will take—you stop only when a condition is met.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style = "font-size: 14pt;"> This same distinction shows up in programming.</p>

</p>
</div>
</details>


---

## What is Iteration?

#### Instead of writing the same instructions over and over, programmers use loops to tell the computer how and when to repeat steps.

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">📖 <strong>Iteration</strong> is the process of repeating a set of steps in a program.
</p>
</div>

<img src="https://images.code.org/585b2b4ff38f158bed045f89829c9864-image-1768429776424.png" alt="icon of looping arrows" width="250px" style="border-radius:10px; float:left; margin: 0px 30px 30px 40px;">

#### In web apps and other programs, iteration is used to:

- #### Check for user input repeatedly

- #### Update information on the screen

- #### Process lists of data

- #### Keep a program running until something changes



<div style="clear:both;"></div>

---

<img src="https://images.code.org/d80a0b774a89fa285465c1a50a654d79-image-1768419690819.png" alt="flow chart for a for loop showing taking shots until it reaches 5" width="650px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; ">

#### Think of it like a container that holds multiple items in a specific order.


- #### Each item has a position number called an **index**.
- #### Index numbering begins with 0.


---

## Next up

#### Let's see what this looks like in code! 

A for loop repeats a set of steps a specific number of times.

For example, this for loop prints out the numbers 1-10:

for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i += 1) {
  console.log(i)
}

![](https://images.code.org/4afcb2bde4184c0c4735dcfb18d4a4a2-image-1768418982827.png)

![](https://images.code.org/585b2b4ff38f158bed045f89829c9864-image-1768429776424.png)
